const e="biblical-olympiad-dominican-republic",h=2024,t="Built from the English translation of the Ministry of Education's 8-page guidelines for the 2023-2024 edition. The text prints no entry fee, no contact person, no phone or email, no exact dates or addresses, no printed age limits and no marks for places. Annex 8 prints Curricular Creativity criteria per work type (4 points each, total 50) but the printed list does not add up to 50, so no per-criterion marks are captured. The classroom stage is a single 100-point written test; later stages are 50 + 50.",a=JSON.parse(`{"Title":"Rules & Criteria","Subtitle":"National school Biblical Olympiad (Olympiad of Integral Human and Religious Formation), Dominican Republic: five stages from classroom to national.","NoticeTitle":"Please Read Before Registering","Notice":"You must have taken part in every earlier stage to enter the next one. Bring your birth certificate copy and the registration form to the district stage. Dates: classroom {{ClassroomStageDates}}, center {{CenterStageDates}}, district {{DistrictStageDates}}, regional {{RegionalStageDates}}, national {{NationalStageDates}}.","Notes":["Each category has its own part of the Bible for the written test.","Curricular Creativity is individual: no companions are allowed.","Dress in the proper school uniform and bring everything your presentation needs."],"Sections":[{"Key":"general","Title":"About the Olympiad","Items":["The Olympiad of Integral Human and Religious Formation (called the Biblical Olympiad in earlier years) is a formative experience that helps students learn and take on values. It is based on the study of biblical texts, the creative presentation of biblical characters and curricular content.","It is organised by the General Directorate of Curriculum through the Area of Integral Human and Religious Formation, with the Department of Impact Programs.","It has four categories and five stages. The categories follow the student's school grade and the stages follow the administrative structure of the Ministry of Education.","In the 2023-2024 school year each category's written test covers one part of the Bible. Curricular themes (the person, the family, play, technology, work, nature, the religious dimension and the person of Jesus) are included in the creative presentation.","Districts and regions must include support and transport for students in their annual operating plans so the Olympiad can be run properly."]},{"Key":"eligibility","Title":"Who can enter","Items":["Category A: students in 5th and 6th grade of the Primary level.","Category B: students in 1st and 2nd grade of the Secondary level.","Category C: students in 3rd and 4th grade of the Secondary level.","Category D: students in 5th and 6th grade of the Secondary level.","Students from public and private schools can take part.","To enter the center stage a student must have completed the classroom stage.","To enter the district stage a student must have completed the classroom and center stages.","To enter the regional stage a student must have completed the classroom, center and district stages.","To enter the national stage a student must have taken part in the classroom, center, district and regional stages.","At the district stage each student must bring a copy of their birth certificate and a form filled in block letters with their data.","The student registration form asks for the school, sector (public, private or semi-official), shift, director, school address, the student's name, place and date of birth, sex, grade, level, phone, email, father, mother or guardian with cell phones, home address and polo shirt size."]},{"Key":"registration","Title":"How the stages work","Items":["The district technicians of the Integral Human and Religious Formation area, with the Impact Programs technician, guide and motivate teachers of public and private schools to prepare for the Olympiad. This orientation is done in September.","Educational centers report to their School District by email or printed document the number of students per category going to the district stage.","Each district reports to its Region the number of participants per category, and each Region sends the Impact Programs Office the complete form with the number of participants per district. The Region reports the regional-stage numbers to the Impact Programs Office by email or in person.","Printed blank registration forms must be available at the district stage for students who have not filled one in.","The district technicians keep in touch with the selected students and teachers to train them with the question bank and the Curricular Creativity criteria supplied by the curricular team. The regional technicians keep in touch with the students selected at the regional stage to support them."]},{"Key":"levels","Title":"Stages and who moves up","Items":["Stage 1, classroom: each classroom teacher runs it. The three students with the highest grade in each category represent their class section at the educational center.","Stage 2, educational center: the one student with the highest grade in each category (A, B, C, D) goes to the district stage.","Stage 3, district: the two students with the highest grade in each category go to the regional stage, eight students in all. The district sends them with a record signed by the District Director, the district Integral Human and Religious Formation technician and the Impact Programs technician.","Stage 4, regional: the two students with the highest score in each category go to the national stage, eight students in all. The Region sends them with a record signed by the regional technician, the Impact Programs technician and the Regional Director.","Stage 5, national: students from the eighteen Regional Education Offices of the country, or those that held the regional stage, take part. The top three places in each category win.","The classroom and center stages are run in the school; the district stage by the Educational District; the regional stage by the Educational Region; the national stage by the central office of the Ministry of Education."]},{"Key":"dates","Title":"Dates","Items":["Classroom stage: {{ClassroomStageDates}}.","Educational center stage: {{CenterStageDates}} (suggested).","District stage: {{DistrictStageDates}}.","Regional stage: {{RegionalStageDates}}.","National stage: {{NationalStageDates}}."]},{"Key":"venue","Title":"Where it is held","Items":["The classroom and center stages are held in the student's own school; the district stage in the Educational District; the regional stage in the Educational Region.","The national stage is held in Santo Domingo, National District. The central office of the Ministry of Education chooses a suitable place: {{NationalVenue}}."]},{"Key":"judging","Title":"Judging and scoring","Items":["Classroom stage: one written test worth 100 points, made by the area teachers from the general item bank of the Olympiad for the category.","Center, district, regional and national stages: a written test worth 50 points plus Curricular Creativity worth 50 points. The two added together are the final grade out of 100.","The winner is the student with the highest total. In a tie, the student who handed in the written test first is chosen, so the jury records the order of delivery in the attendance register.","The suggested jury at the center, district and regional stages is a teacher of Integral Human and Religious Formation, a Spanish Language teacher, an Arts Education teacher, a priest, pastor or religious man or woman, and a teaching coordinator or the school director. The regional technicians with the district technicians choose the jury for the district and regional stages. The jury applies and corrects the written test.","At the national stage the area's curricular team chooses the jury with the Impact Programs team. It is suggested to be made up of priests, pastors, national, regional and district technicians, teachers of this area, Arts Education teachers and Spanish Language teachers. The jury chooses the questions from the item bank, then applies and corrects the test.","Each stage records its results on a score sheet (classroom, center, district, regional and national record). The sheets are kept in the Annexes of the Guidelines.","A winners' record (minutes of winners) is signed for the national stage."]},{"Key":"points","Title":"Places and points","Items":["No points are printed for places.","At the national stage the top three places in each category (A, B, C, D) are the winners."]},{"Key":"prizes","Title":"Prizes and recognition","Items":["The classroom teacher and the school management team decide the awards at the classroom stage, and the area teachers and school management team at the center stage. The Educational District provides the awards at the district stage and the Educational Region at the regional stage. The central office of the Ministry provides the awards at the national stage.","At the national stage the top three students of each category win and two honorable mentions per category are given to the 4th and 5th place students. In total there are 20 winning students.","Only students who placed in the categories and one companion attend the award ceremony."]},{"Key":"payments","Title":"Transport and support","Items":["The center covers the transport of each student and accompanying teachers to the Educational District.","The Educational District covers the transport of each student and the accompanying technician to the Educational Region.","The Educational Region covers the transport of each student and the accompanying technician to Santo Domingo, National District. Each Region plans two trips: one for taking the test, with eleven people (eight students and three companions), and a second for the awards ceremony.","The funds for transport from the districts to the regions and from the regions to Santo Domingo must be in the annual operating plan of each office.","The Educational District provides support materials, consumables, a snack and the awards at the district stage. The Educational Region provides them at the regional stage. At the national stage the central office also provides the lunch.","Parents, guardians and members of the community are invited to attend the district, regional and national stages."]},{"Key":"conduct","Title":"Conduct","Items":["Students must take part properly uniformed at every stage, and at the national stage on both occasions.","Students bring all resources their presentation needs: costumes, music, make-up, banners, murals and anything else.","Monologues, songs, poems and decimas written by others are not graded under the Curricular Creativity criteria; students must write their own."]},{"Key":"reporting","Title":"Records and forms","Items":["Each stage keeps a score record with: the student, the order of delivery of the written test, the written test (50 points), Curricular Creativity (50 points) and the final grade (100).","The district sends the Region a record of winners signed by three officials, and each Region sends the Impact Programs Office a signed record.","Forms in the Guidelines: student registration form, district-to-regional record, number of students per district, number of students and centers per region, regional-to-Impact-Programs record, Curricular Creativity description form, Curricular Creativity evaluation form, presentation program, winners' record, score records per stage and the winners' minutes."]}],"Documents":[{"Title":"Guidelines on the Biblical Olympiad 2023-2024 (English translation)","Url":"https://www.scribd.com/document/954796745/Guidelines-on-the-Biblical-Olympiad-2023-2024-2-4"}]}`),n=[],i=[{AgeGroup:"Category A",Source:"Guidelines for the Olympics of the Integral Human and Religious Formation Area 2023-2024, I. General Guidelines",Sections:{About:["Grades: 5th and 6th of Primary.","Written test topic: Biblical Generalities and the Books of the Pentateuch."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:null,Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Grades: 5th and 6th of Primary.
- Written test topic: Biblical Generalities and the Books of the Pentateuch.`},{AgeGroup:"Category B",Source:"Guidelines for the Olympics of the Integral Human and Religious Formation Area 2023-2024, I. General Guidelines",Sections:{About:["Grades: 1st and 2nd of Secondary.","Written test topic: the Historical and Prophetic Books."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:null,Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Grades: 1st and 2nd of Secondary.
- Written test topic: the Historical and Prophetic Books.`},{AgeGroup:"Category C",Source:"Guidelines for the Olympics of the Integral Human and Religious Formation Area 2023-2024, I. General Guidelines",Sections:{About:["Grades: 3rd and 4th of Secondary.","Written test topic: the Gospels and Acts of the Apostles."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:null,Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Grades: 3rd and 4th of Secondary.
- Written test topic: the Gospels and Acts of the Apostles.`},{AgeGroup:"Category D",Source:"Guidelines for the Olympics of the Integral Human and Religious Formation Area 2023-2024, I. General Guidelines",Sections:{About:["Grades: 5th and 6th of Secondary.","Written test topic: the Pauline Letters and the Apocalypse."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:null,Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Grades: 5th and 6th of Secondary.
- Written test topic: the Pauline Letters and the Apocalypse.`}],o=[{Title:"Biblical Written Test",Source:"Guidelines for the Olympics of the Integral Human and Religious Formation Area 2023-2024, sections 2.1 to 2.5",Sections:{About:["The test covers the part of the Bible set for the student's category."],TimeAndFormat:["Worth 100 points at the classroom stage and 50 points at the center, district, regional and national stages.","The test is made from the general item bank of the Olympiad. Multiple-choice, completion, and analysis of biblical texts and characters are suggested."],Rules:["The jury records the order of delivery of each student in the attendance register. In a tie, the student who handed in first is chosen.","The teacher is advised to record the order of submission at the classroom stage too.","Participants are given the bank of questions about the biblical content to study for the classroom stage."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:["The written test and the Curricular Creativity are added to give the final grade out of 100 from the center stage on."],Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- The test covers the part of the Bible set for the student's category.

## Time and format
- Worth 100 points at the classroom stage and 50 points at the center, district, regional and national stages.
- The test is made from the general item bank of the Olympiad. Multiple-choice, completion, and analysis of biblical texts and characters are suggested.

## Rules
- The jury records the order of delivery of each student in the attendance register. In a tie, the student who handed in first is chosen.
- The teacher is advised to record the order of submission at the classroom stage too.
- Participants are given the bank of questions about the biblical content to study for the classroom stage.

## Judging
- The written test and the Curricular Creativity are added to give the final grade out of 100 from the center stage on.`},{Title:"Curricular Creativity",Source:"Guidelines for the Olympics of the Integral Human and Religious Formation Area 2023-2024, sections 2.2 to 2.5 and Annexes 7 to 9",Sections:{About:["An artistic presentation done after the written test, from the center stage on. Not part of the classroom stage."],TimeAndFormat:["Maximum 5 minutes.","Worth 50 points."],Rules:["Curricular Creativity is individual: no companions are allowed.","The student picks a content or theme from the curriculum of their grade, or a character, a book, a theme or a biblical scene from the part of the Bible worked on in the written test.","It is presented as a monologue, song, poem or decima.","The students must write their own work. Work written by others is not graded under the evaluation criteria.","Before the presentation the student gives the jury a document describing the work: type, title, objective and description (what it is about, messages or teachings, values it conveys and the biblical aspect it deals with).","A program of all the artistic presentations, one per category with student names, grade, type, title and duration, must be with the jury when the students present.","The student wears the proper uniform and brings the costumes, music, make-up, banners and murals needed."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:["The jury fills in one evaluation form for each participant, using the criteria for the type of work (monologue, poetry, song or decima).","Annex 8 says the total score is 50 points and each criterion is worth 4 points."],Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- An artistic presentation done after the written test, from the center stage on. Not part of the classroom stage.

## Time and format
- Maximum 5 minutes.
- Worth 50 points.

## Rules
- Curricular Creativity is individual: no companions are allowed.
- The student picks a content or theme from the curriculum of their grade, or a character, a book, a theme or a biblical scene from the part of the Bible worked on in the written test.
- It is presented as a monologue, song, poem or decima.
- The students must write their own work. Work written by others is not graded under the evaluation criteria.
- Before the presentation the student gives the jury a document describing the work: type, title, objective and description (what it is about, messages or teachings, values it conveys and the biblical aspect it deals with).
- A program of all the artistic presentations, one per category with student names, grade, type, title and duration, must be with the jury when the students present.
- The student wears the proper uniform and brings the costumes, music, make-up, banners and murals needed.

## Judging
- The jury fills in one evaluation form for each participant, using the criteria for the type of work (monologue, poetry, song or decima).
- Annex 8 says the total score is 50 points and each criterion is worth 4 points.`}],r=[],s=[{Key:"ClassroomStageDates",Kind:"date",Label:"Classroom stage dates",Value:"September-October 2023",Source:"Guidelines for the Olympics of the Integral Human and Religious Formation Area 2023-2024, 2.1"},{Key:"CenterStageDates",Kind:"date",Label:"Educational center stage dates",Value:"November-December 2023",Source:"Guidelines for the Olympics of the Integral Human and Religious Formation Area 2023-2024, 2.2"},{Key:"DistrictStageDates",Kind:"date",Label:"District stage dates",Value:"January-February 2024",Source:"Guidelines for the Olympics of the Integral Human and Religious Formation Area 2023-2024, 2.3"},{Key:"RegionalStageDates",Kind:"date",Label:"Regional stage dates",Value:"March-April 2024",Source:"Guidelines for the Olympics of the Integral Human and Religious Formation Area 2023-2024, 2.4"},{Key:"NationalStageDates",Kind:"date",Label:"National stage dates",Value:"May 2024",Source:"Guidelines for the Olympics of the Integral Human and Religious Formation Area 2023-2024, 2.5"},{Key:"NationalVenue",Kind:"address",Label:"National stage venue",Value:{Name:"To be announced",Address:"",City:"Santo Domingo, National District, Dominican Republic",MapUrl:"",Directions:""},Source:"Guidelines for the Olympics of the Integral Human and Religious Formation Area 2023-2024, 2.5"}],l=[{Claim:"Stages, eligibility, qualification quotas, scoring and award rules are printed in the organiser's guidelines.",SourceType:"Primary - the event's own rules document",Source:"Guidelines for the Olympics of the Integral Human and Religious Formation Area 2023-2024",Url:"https://www.scribd.com/document/954796745/Guidelines-on-the-Biblical-Olympiad-2023-2024-2-4"},{Claim:"The guidelines print no entry fee, contact details, exact dates or address for the venue, so none are captured.",SourceType:"Scope note - nothing printed to build from",Source:"Guidelines for the Olympics of the Integral Human and Religious Formation Area 2023-2024",Url:"https://www.scribd.com/document/954796745/Guidelines-on-the-Biblical-Olympiad-2023-2024-2-4"}],c={TemplateKey:e,Year:2024,Note:t,Page:a,ParticipantTypes:n,AgeGroups:i,Competitions:o,CompetitionAgeGroups:r,Variables:s,Evidence:l};export{i as AgeGroups,r as CompetitionAgeGroups,o as Competitions,l as Evidence,t as Note,a as Page,n as ParticipantTypes,e as TemplateKey,s as Variables,h as Year,c as default};
